\f1\fs22 Sarah lay on the examination table, her hand unconsciously protective over her belly as Kathy prepared the ultrasound. The room felt more like the inside of a toy box, perhaps because of the additional monitoring equipment Kathy had crammed into it. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Everything feeling normal?\'92 Kathy asked.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Nothing about this is normal,\'92 Sarah said with the tone Sarah reserved for asking good friends to up their game.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy nodded, spreading gel across Sarah's abdomen. \'91Let's see what Little Clinton\'92s up to today.\'92 She applied the ultrasound\'92s transducer and circled it around a little while she studied its screen. She relaxed a little. \'91Well he 
\f2\i looks
\f1\i0  normal.\'92 She set down the transducer, and wiped the gel off Sarah\'92s belly.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy helped Sarah sit up, and noted her troubled expression. \'91Are you feeling OK?\'92 Certainly Sarah appeared healthy enough.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah blinked. \'91Look. I\'92m usually the last one to complain about anything,\'92 she said, taking her time, as if she was unsure which words her mouth would say next. \'91And this \'85 well, it\'92s stupid.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy gave her an attentive stare and the time to arrange her thoughts.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Well, I\'92ve never been sick a day in my life, at least since I was seven or eight years old.\'92 Sarah shook her head. Those days were hauntingly dim for her. \'91Even so, I feel even healthier than I\'92ve ever been.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91And that\'92s your complaint?\'92 Kathy was only moderately trying to make light of it.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I know, it\'92s stupid. But I feel like there\'92s something magical about Little Clinton.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Spoken like every mother ever.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'92No. I mean \'85\'92 Sarah searched for the foundations of how she felt. \'91It\'92s like he\'92s the one nurturing 
\f2\i me
\f1\i0 . No, it\'92s more than that. It\'92s like he 
\f2\i knows
\f1\i0  what I need.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy felt a sharp need for a patch at that point. Quite suddenly, she missed Tony. \'91You know, Tony kept saying Little Clinton was the influence for all his song-writing.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah snorted in perfectly feminine manner. \'91Well, I have not know Mr. Fairy Boxers for long, but it seems the deepest thing about him is his apparent shallowness.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy slapped Sarah\'92s wrist. \'91Don\'92t you go dissing my old mate Tony. There\'92s not a devious bone in his body. Well, except for his bone, which bananas extravagantly to the right.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I would know nothing about that,\'92 Sarah said sternly.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91So, not the father, then.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91What? No! Certainly not.\'92 Sarah shuddered but then felt the sudden melt into a frisson altogether more invigorating.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I only ask, because \'85 well, he\'92s always, been so attentive toward the little fella. In the beginning, I thought he was just obsessed with you, and to be frank, who wouldn\'92t be?\'92 Kathy pulled out of that spiral of thoughts by picturing Hayley. \'91But it\'92s more than that, he\'92s obsessed with both of you.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah grew impatient. \'91I had no say in this whatsoever. I told you, those sage gnomes impregnated me.\'92 Didn\'92t they? She still had her doubts. \'91You don\'92t think Tony \'85 I slept like the dead that night we shared a room after the Vegas Show.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>It was Kathy\'92s turn to become indignant. \'91Yet again with the dissing Tony? He\'92d never be like that. He\'92s one of the decent ones, you know?\'92 Kathy really needed a patch.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Deep in her heart, Sarah did know that. \'91So what now?\'92 She asked, though something in Kathy's expression suggested she already had a plan.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy moved to a covered apparatus in the corner. To Sarah it look like a large white M&M character from the ads that was reclining back at an angle and had its mouth open in a wide \'91O\'92.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy patted it. \'91This one, curiously, is called SARA\'97Superconducting Array for Reproductive Assessment.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah shivered as she realised the open mouth was large enough to suck on her distended belly.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy wheeled it closer. \'91Nearly two hundred SQUID sensors,\'92 she explained.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I hope you\'92re. Not saying my baby is a squid.\'92 Sarah was equal parts joking and horrified.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91No. SQUID is an acronym. Superconducting Quantum Interference Device.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Oh good, that makes me feel much better.\'92 Sarah wondered why medical equipment designers always took their aesthetic inspiration from 
\f2\i Ulyssian House and Dungeon
\f1\i0 . The thing looked like a cross between an oversized egg cup and a Johnny Ive inspired torture device. \'91Is it safe?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Oh, it\'92s got an app!\'92 Kathy effused and held up her tablet. \'91It\'92s completely passive. It only detects, doesn't emit. The magnetic fields generated by neural activity are incredibly weak\'97billionths of a tesla. But SARA can pick them up. If your baby's brain is doing something extraordinary, this will show us.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91So, I\'85?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Hop off the table,\'92 Kathy said happily, \'91You kneel on the pads there either side, and just let you belly\'97\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Flop in the M&M\'92s belly.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91There you go,\'92 Kathy continued. \'91Let you arms drape over the runners here and your head rests on the padding between.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah did as she was instructed. To her surprise, engaging with the contraptions was almost comfortable.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The moment Kathy activated the system, the attached display console erupted with data. Where the ultrasound had shown nothing unusual, SARA revealed waves of neural activity unlike anything in the medical literature.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy took a breath and leaned closer to the console monitor. \'91These patterns\'85\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah\'92s mind\'92s eye was overcome with complex geometric patterns\'97spirals and interconnected nodes that seemed to follow a mathematical logic. They pulsed with purposeful rhythm, creating interference patterns that looked almost like language.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah gasped and pushed herself out of the contraption\'92s cradle, keeling over it with frozen kneels and elbows. She blinked. Hard. And coughed.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I just saw, while I was in that thing,  the same patterns I\'92ve been dreaming, only clearer and more vivd. They\'92re like the scars in that bunker that Johnny blew up.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy punched a few buttons and twiddled a dial which began a replay of on the console monitor. \'91You might like this?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah looked over her should at the screen. The images were not as detailed as the flash she received. \'91Uh, yeah.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy helped Sarah to stand and patted the examination table to sit.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I feel it, Kathy. Little Clinton. He\'92s not just developing. He\'92s\'85 preparing. For something.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>With her her hands gently on Sarah shoulders, she urged her to lie back.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91What\'92s happening, Kathy?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I don\'92t know,\'92 Kathy confessed. \'91All I can say is that Little Clinton is\'85 extraordinary.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Is he going to be ok?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>While Kathy wouldn\'92t buy a mother\'92s unscientific assessment of her baby possessing the ability to \'93prepare\'94, whatever that meant, the coherence of the readings she\'92d seen on console monitor, as  indecipherable as she found them, suggested health and vitality more than illness. \'91Yes, he\'92s going to be fine,\'92 she answered with more conviction than she felt. \'91He\'92s going to be more than fine.\'92 That felt truer.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Panic set in for Sarah. She grabbed Kathy with claw-like hands. \'91Kathy, we can\'92t tell Eloise of this. No one can know,\'92 she pleaded. \'91He\'92s just a baby. Eloise will want to exploit him.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy feared that may have been Eloise\'92s plan all the long.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91My baby is not a specimen for research,\'92 Sarah commanded.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy stared at the SARA readouts for a long time. The data were beautiful and terrifying\'97perhaps a glimpse of humanity's next chapter, written in magnetic fields by an unborn mind that was already reaching past the boundaries of its current form.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I need people who see my child as a person, not a specimen.\'92 Sarah's voice was fierce despite her exhaustion. \'91You know what happens when this gets out.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy swallowed. What these tests needed more than anything was more tests. Then there\'92d be reports, committees, hungry political eyes.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy flipped the machine off, and wheeled it back into the corner. If Eloise cared to look, she\'92d see the equipment Kathy requisitioned. Eloise would be curious, she\'92d demand clear, objective, persuasive answers. Kathy would have to lit to her, and lie well.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Kathy?\'92 Sarah watched her friend\'92s mood turn solemn, watched her retrieve her handbag from the side-table and fossick around in in its depths, watched her pull out a patch, peel of its protective layer, then apply it too her shoulder.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Tax it,\'92 Kathy said. \'91I can lie to the best of them.\'92
